---
import Layout from '@layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="How to Book and Ride China High-Speed Rail" lang="en">
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto py-12">
    <header class="mb-10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 text-sm text-gray-500 mb-4">
        <a href="/en/transport/" class="text-brand-600 hover:underline">Transport</a>
        <span>&rarr;</span>
        <span>Guide</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight mb-4">
        How to Book and Ride China High-Speed Rail
      </h1>
      <p class="text-lg text-gray-500">
        Step-by-step guide to booking tickets, navigating the station, and riding high-speed trains in China.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose prose-gray max-w-none">
      <p>China's high-speed rail network is the largest in the world, connecting nearly every major city. For travelers, it's often the best way to get between cities &#8212; faster than flying when you factor in airport travel time, and far more comfortable than a bus.</p>

      <h2>Booking Your Ticket</h2>
      <p>You have three options:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Trip.com (recommended):</strong> English interface, accepts foreign credit cards, small booking fee (&#165;15-30). You'll get a QR code ticket in the app.</li>
        <li><strong>12306 (official):</strong> Cheaper (no booking fee) but the app is in Chinese only. Foreigners can register with a passport number.</li>
        <li><strong>Station ticket counter:</strong> You can buy tickets in person, but queues can be long and English is not guaranteed.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>For most travelers, Trip.com is the easiest option. The booking fee is worth the convenience.</p>

      <h2>Ticket Classes</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Second Class (&#20108;&#31561;&#24231;):</strong> The standard option. Comfortable seats, 3+2 configuration. This is what most people choose.</li>
        <li><strong>First Class (&#19968;&#31561;&#24231;):</strong> Wider seats, 2+2 configuration, more legroom. Worth upgrading for journeys over 3 hours.</li>
        <li><strong>Business Class (&#21830;&#21153;&#24231;):</strong> Fully reclining seats, privacy, complimentary meals on long routes. Expensive but an experience.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>At the Station</h2>
      <p>Arrive 30-40 minutes before departure. Here's the flow:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Security check:</strong> Bags go through an X-ray machine (like at the airport but faster).</li>
        <li><strong>Waiting hall:</strong> Check the big screen for your train's gate number. Follow the signs.</li>
        <li><strong>Boarding:</strong> Show your passport or QR code at the gate. You'll walk down to the platform.</li>
        <li><strong>Find your carriage:</strong> Platform floor markers show which carriage number you're at. Match the number on your ticket.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Onboard Tips</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Food and drinks are sold from a trolley (instant noodles are surprisingly popular).</li>
        <li>Power outlets are under the seat (Chinese sockets &#8212; bring an adapter).</li>
        <li>WiFi is available on most G-trains but requires a Chinese phone number to log in.</li>
        <li>Announcements are in Chinese and English.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="mt-8 p-4 bg-brand-50 rounded-xl">
        <strong>Important:</strong> Your passport is your ticket. Keep it handy for checks at the station entrance, boarding gate, and sometimes on the train. Don't put it in your checked luggage.
      </p>

      <p><a href="/en/transport/transport-guide/">&larr; Back to the complete transport guide</a></p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
